+++ b/tools/video/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,212 @@
+# Video workload profiles for Linux 7.2.6
+
+These tools configure this patched x86-64 Linux 7.2.6 source tree and launch
+video workloads with task-scoped resource controls. The configuration base is
+an explicit input. Its kernel version does not change the target source version.
+For example, `/proc/config.gz` can supply a running 7.2.2 kernel's hardware
+configuration while the output targets 7.2.6.
+
+The settings are measurement candidates, not demonstrated performance gains.
+BORE, POC, compiler optimization, device drivers, and trust settings come from
+the base configuration. No scheduler, memory-management, or GPU-driver algorithm
+is changed by this package.
+
+## Generate a configuration
+
+Run from the kernel source root:
+
+```sh
+make -C tools/video configure BASE=/proc/config.gz PROFILE=interactive
+make -C tools/video configure BASE=/proc/config.gz PROFILE=throughput
+```
+
+The default outputs are `out/video-interactive/.config` and
+`out/video-throughput/.config`. An existing nonempty output directory is refused.
+Use an explicit saved configuration instead of `/proc/config.gz` to reproduce
+a previous result. Plain and gzip-compressed configurations are supported.
+
+```sh
+make -C tools/video configure \
+    BASE=/path/to/saved.config \
+    PROFILE=interactive \
+    OUTPUT=/path/to/new-output \
+    LLVM=-22
+```
+
+The configuration workflow requires the normal Kconfig host-tool prerequisites
+and an installed LLVM toolchain. It compiles Kconfig host utilities and runs
+configuration targets; it does not compile, install, or boot the kernel.
+`LLVM=-22` is the default. The source-root `.config` is not created or replaced.
+
+| Kernel configuration profile | Initial default | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `interactive` | Full preemption, 1000 Hz | Editing, preview, capture, and streaming latency experiments |
+| `throughput` | Lazy preemption, 250 Hz | Batch encoding throughput experiments |
+
+Both profiles enable dynamic preemption, CPU/cpuset/memory/block cgroup controls,
+utilization clamping, PSI, MGLRU support, existing tracing interfaces, and shared
+buffer synchronization. MGLRU activation follows the base. Existing DRM/media
+module-versus-builtin choices are retained. Device-specific media drivers must
+already be present in the base or configured for the actual capture hardware.
+Utilization clamping is a scheduler hint; its frequency effect depends on the
+active CPU frequency driver and mode.
+
+On this x86 source, `ARCH_HAS_PREEMPT_LAZY` excludes both Kconfig and runtime
+`voluntary` selection. The dynamic alternatives are `full` and `lazy`.
+No boot parameters or running preemption settings are changed by these tools.
+
+Each output retains:
+
+- `base.config` and `video-base-derived.config` for reproduction.
+- `provenance.txt` with source version, toolchain, commands, and input checksums.
+- `merge.log`, `kconfig.log`, and `config.diff` for review.
+- `kernel.release` and `listnewconfig.log` for release and configuration checks.
+
+The script verifies every requested fragment value after Kconfig resolves
+dependencies. It adds `-video-interactive` or `-video-throughput` to the existing
+metadata revision, or to the local version when metadata is disabled, and checks
+the 64-character kernel-release limit. This distinguishes the two candidate
+kernels' module directories. Changing other build settings still requires a
+distinct revision when simultaneous installation is intended.
+
+Review `config.diff` before building. Hardware compatibility and successful
+Kconfig resolution do not prove compilation or bootability. In particular, a
+base's `CONFIG_SYSTEM_TRUSTED_KEYS` file must exist in the target source or use
+the configured absolute path. Do not remove trusted keys to conceal a missing
+certificate. The captured base uses `certs/dkms-mok.pem`; provision the intended
+public certificate before a kernel build. External NVIDIA modules must be built
+and validated for the resulting kernel release separately.
+
+## Run a workload
+
+The launcher requires Bash, systemd with `--expand-environment=no` support
+(version 254 or later), a user manager, and cgroup v2 at `/sys/fs/cgroup`.
+It uses a transient scope under the user manager's `app.slice` and preserves the
+working directory, environment, argument boundaries, and workload exit status.
+
+```sh
+tools/video/run.sh --dry-run interactive -- ffmpeg -version
+tools/video/run.sh interactive -- /path/to/editor
+tools/video/run.sh throughput -- ffmpeg -i input.mkv -c:v libx265 output.mkv
+tools/video/run.sh background -- /path/to/background-job
+```
+
+| Execution profile | CPU weight | Interpretation |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| `interactive` | 200 | Higher relative CPU share under contention |
+| `throughput` | 100 | Normal relative CPU share |
+| `background` | 25 | Lower relative CPU share under contention |
+
+These are relative weights among sibling cgroups, not CPU reservations,
+frequency settings, real-time scheduling classes, or GPU priorities. An idle
+system does not reserve unused CPU time for a higher-weight scope. The execution
+profile does not select the kernel configuration profile or change its tick
+frequency. Programs that hand work to an already-running service may execute
+that work outside the new scope.
+
+CPU placement and memory protection are explicit options:
+
+```sh
+tools/video/run.sh --memory-low 2G interactive -- /path/to/editor
+tools/video/run.sh --cpus 0-3 throughput -- /path/to/encoder
+```
+
+The CPU list is an example, not a mapping to this machine's P cores. Obtain the
+actual topology before selecting CPUs. The optional `--cpus` argument uses
+the existing `taskset` command to set process affinity without requiring a
+delegated cpuset controller. The effective affinity must match the request;
+offline CPUs or ancestor restrictions cause a pre-execution failure. This is
+process affinity, not cpuset containment: child processes inherit it, but the
+workload and its descendants can subsequently change their affinity.
+Memory values use binary K/M/G units or bytes and must be page-aligned.
+`memory.low` is best-effort reclaim protection, not a memory allocation,
+reservation, or hard limit. Ancestor protection and contention determine its
+effective benefit. No memory protection or CPU affinity is added by default.
+
+I/O control requires explicit opt-in and delegation:
+
+```sh
+tools/video/run.sh --io-weight 200 interactive -- /path/to/editor
+```
+
+The launcher verifies `cpu.weight`, requested `io.weight`, requested
+`memory.low`, and requested process affinity through `/proc/self/status` inside the scope before
+executing the workload. It fails if a requested control is unavailable. A user
+manager may lack delegated I/O control even when the kernel supports it; the
+default invocation therefore does not request an I/O weight. Successful
+`io.weight` read-back does not prove that the active device/scheduler uses it.
+Verify device-level fairness under contention before drawing conclusions.
+
+Scope settings disappear when all processes in the scope exit. No files are
+installed, and no global sysctl, BORE switch, preemption mode, I/O scheduler,
+cache, clock policy, or memory tunable is changed. Detached child processes keep
+the scope alive until they exit. The launcher prints the scope name for inspection
+or termination through `systemctl --user`.
+
+## Measure and compare
+
+`benchmark.sh` runs an exact command five times by default. The output directory
+must not exist. It captures per-run stdout/stderr, millisecond-resolution elapsed
+time, exit status, PSI snapshots, readable global tuning state, and optional
+bounded NVIDIA inventory. A failing command stops the run and retains evidence.
+
+```sh
+tools/video/benchmark.sh --output /tmp/video-software-baseline --runs 5 -- \
+    ffmpeg -nostdin -i /path/to/fixed-input.mkv \
+    -map 0:v:0 -an -c:v libx265 -preset medium -crf 22 -f null -
+
+tools/video/benchmark.sh --output /tmp/video-software-profile --runs 5 -- \
+    tools/video/run.sh throughput -- \
+    ffmpeg -nostdin -i /path/to/fixed-input.mkv \
+    -map 0:v:0 -an -c:v libx265 -preset medium -crf 22 -f null -
+```
+
+The null-output example measures encoding without output-file storage. Use a
+separate file-output experiment for muxing/writeback behavior. Use identical
+input, codec, quality, thread count, power state, and background load. Warm up
+consistently and alternate baseline/candidate order. Command startup and scope
+creation are included in elapsed time. Kernel configuration comparisons require
+building and booting each candidate; running these tools on an older kernel does
+not measure the 7.2.6 candidate.
+
+`results.tsv` and `summary.txt` contain elapsed time only. They do not infer
+encoded frame rate, frame latency, output quality, power, or correctness.
+Application progress logs, frame timestamps, dropped-frame counters, decoded
+output checks, and power/temperature samples must supply those measurements.
+Do not use aggregate system PSI snapshots as per-process attribution.
+
+Compare one variable at a time: BORE on/off, POC configuration, full/lazy
+preemption, tick rate, MGLRU, and CPU placement. The two supplied kernel profiles
+change both tick rate and preemption, so a direct comparison evaluates the
+combined candidate rather than either individual mechanism. BORE-off in a
+patched kernel is not identical to an unpatched scheduler. Do not mix a new
+compiler optimization or GPU-driver change into the same experiment.
+
+Accept changes only when improvement exceeds run-to-run variation and the
+agreed regression budget. Check encoding time, frame p95/p99 latency, frame
+drops, audio continuity, GPU errors, game frame times, and desktop response.
+Capture the same workload under scheduler/block/memory tracepoints before
+proposing an algorithm patch. Hardware video acceleration also depends on the
+application, codec, driver, and frame-transfer path; enabling DMA-BUF does not
+make an arbitrary pipeline zero-copy.
+
+## Tool checks
+
+```sh
+make -C tools/video check
+```
+
+The check target requires ShellCheck as a development tool. It checks syntax,
+invalid-input rejection, output protection, argument preservation, profile
+construction, failure propagation, and benchmark artifacts. Real cgroup
+read-back and kernel configuration checks are reported separately in
+`VALIDATION.md`. No performance improvement is established by these checks.
+
+## References
+
+- [Kernel cgroup v2 resource semantics](https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html).
+- [Kernel MGLRU interface](https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/multigen_lru.html).
+- [Kernel DMA-BUF synchronization](https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/dma-buf.html).
+- [systemd-run](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd-run.html)
+  and [resource controls](https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.resource-control.html).
+- [NVIDIA FFmpeg hardware acceleration](https://docs.nvidia.com/video-technologies/video-codec-sdk/13.0/ffmpeg-with-nvidia-gpu/index.html).
